## [ISSUE] `join()` never terminates flusher processes, and there are now N of them

**Severity:** LOW — after a timed-out join on rebalance, flusher processes can keep flushing shards that may now belong to another consumer.

### The Problem

```python
for process_index, process in self.processes.items():
    if deadline is not None:
        remaining_time = deadline - time.time()
        if remaining_time <= 0:
            break

    while process.is_alive() and (deadline is None or deadline > time.time()):
        time.sleep(0.1)

    if isinstance(process, multiprocessing.Process):
        process.terminate()
```

Processes are created with `multiprocessing.get_context("spawn").Process` (lines 53, 105). That gives a `SpawnProcess`, whose MRO is `SpawnProcess -> BaseProcess -> object`, so `isinstance(process, multiprocessing.Process)` is False and `terminate()` never runs. The `break` skips the remaining processes, but they would not have been terminated anyway.

### Theory / Proof

In CPython `Lib/multiprocessing/context.py`, `Process` and `SpawnProcess` are siblings that both subclass `process.BaseProcess`. When the deadline passes, every flusher (whether or not the loop reached it) stops only when it next reads `stopped.value` at the top of its loop (line 166). Arroyo calls `join(timeout)` on partition revocation and then `create_with_partitions` for the new assignment. So a process that is in the middle of a long `flush_segments`/produce keeps working on shards that may now be owned by another consumer.

### What happens today vs. what will break

The single-process code before this PR had the same dead check. This PR makes it N stray processes instead of one. It is related to the restart-path problem at line 254 (where this PR also dropped the unconditional `kill()`).

### Research

`multiprocessing.Pool.terminate` and `ProcessPoolExecutor.shutdown` wait on all workers against one deadline, then force-stop the ones that are still alive.

### Recommendation

```python
for process in self.processes.values():
    while process.is_alive() and (deadline is None or deadline > time.time()):
        time.sleep(0.1)

for process in self.processes.values():
    if isinstance(process, multiprocessing.process.BaseProcess) and process.is_alive():
        process.terminate()
```
